From ee1f2cd0cb2e4f762b7a79a0baed1511a1fbf364 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeff Daily <jeff.daily@amd.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 02:47:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] [ROCm] Skip Warp graph capture on devices that cannot
 capture

Warp built for ROCm reports HIP devices as CUDA devices (`Device.is_cuda` is True) but cannot capture graphs on them: `Device.supports_graph_capture` is False, `wp.ScopedCapture` runs its body eagerly and leaves `capture.graph` as None, and `wp.capture_launch(None)` raises. Isaac Lab gates every capture on `is_cuda`, so on AMD GPUs the Newton backend ran each physics step twice (once eagerly, once inside the capture that produced no graph) and recaptured on every step, and the BVH refit, ray caster, frame transformer, Warp renderer, Newton actuator runtime, particle surface, IK and `WarpGraphCache` paths raised on replay. On an MI300X, 50 `sim.step()` calls ran the physics step 100 times before this change and 50 times after it.

Add `isaaclab.utils.warp.utils.supports_graph_capture(device)`, which is `device.is_cuda` and the device's `supports_graph_capture` attribute, defaulting to True for Warp builds without it, and use it at each capture site so those paths run eagerly instead. The CUDA behavior is unchanged: NVIDIA Warp either lacks the attribute or reports True.

Tests that capture now call the new `isaaclab.test.utils.skip_without_graph_capture(device)` immediately before capturing, so their eager checks still run and only the capture part is skipped. A test that mocks capture patches the check instead, and the MuJoCo Warp reset test falls back to its eager reset.

Also record the HIP version in the GPU info benchmark recorder when PyTorch is a ROCm build, and document a ROCm install for the Newton workflows in the installation guide.

This change was prepared with the help of an AI coding agent.

Test Plan:

On an AMD Instinct MI300X (gfx942) with PyTorch 2.11.0+rocm7.13.0 and Warp 1.18.0.dev2 built for HIP, installed as in the new installation section with the `test` and `importers` extras:

```bash
for f in source/isaaclab/test/sim/test_mjcf_converter.py source/isaaclab/test/sim/test_urdf_converter.py source/isaaclab/test/sim/test_schemas_deprecation.py source/isaaclab_newton/test/assets/test_articulation.py source/isaaclab_newton/test/assets/test_rigid_object.py source/isaaclab_newton/test/assets/test_bam_actuator_mjwarp.py source/isaaclab_newton/test/assets/test_newton_actuators_newton.py source/isaaclab_tasks/test/test_microduck_velocity.py; do python -m pytest -m kitless "$f"; done
for f in source/isaaclab/test/utils/test_warp_math.py source/isaaclab/test/utils/test_warp_memory.py source/isaaclab/test/utils/test_wrench_composer.py source/isaaclab/test/sensors/test_shared_sensor_kernels.py source/isaaclab_newton/test/physics/test_newton_manager.py source/isaaclab_newton/test/physics/test_newton_solvers.py source/isaaclab_experimental/test/envs/mdp/test_*_warp_parity.py; do python -m pytest "$f"; done
isaaclab train --rl_library rsl_rl --task Isaac-Cartpole-Direct --num_envs 4096 --max_iterations 100 physics=newton_mjwarp
python tools/changelog/cli.py check upstream/main
```

All pass except two VBD cases in `test_rigid_object.py`, which fail to link a Newton VBD kernel that calls `nextafterf` on HIP. Cartpole training reaches a mean reward of 4.94 with the 300-step episode length cap.
